Abstract
The development of efficient hydrogen evolution and oxygen evolution reactions bifunctional electrocatalyst for overall water splitting is highly desired but still a great challenge, especially under neutral condition. With the unique properties of polyoxometalate and MOFs materials as well as rich transition metal contents, herein we successfully synthesize a novel bi-phase structure of cobalt and molybdenum carbide coated with nitrogen-doped graphite $\mathbf{\left.Co-Mo_{2}C@N C\right.}$ ) which possesses excellent activity as water splitting electrocatalyst at neutral pH. This noble metal-free, bi-phase electrocatalyst exhibits Hydrogen Evolution Reaction (HER) and Oxygen Evolution Reaction (OER) overpotentials of $260~\mathrm{mV}$ and $440~\mathrm{mV}$ at 10 mA cm−2, respectively. The two-electrode system using $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ as both the anode and cathode drives $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at a cell voltage of $1.83\mathrm{~V~}$ with a remarkable long-term stability. Besides, the $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ also shows promising activity in alkaline condition that reaches $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a cell voltage of 1.66 V. This work paves a new avenue to the design of the unique, economic and promising non-noble metal electrode materials for practical applications in the electrochemical energy storage and conversion devices.
Keywords: poms; ZIF; water splitting; bifunctional catalysis; neutral pH.
## 1. Introduction
As a sustainable, green and safe method of producing hydrogen fuel, electrocatalytic water splitting into hydrogen and oxygen has drawn much attention.1-5 For the overall water splitting reactions, highly efficient and active electrocatalysts are needed for either anodic oxygen evolution (OER) or cathodic hydrogen evolution (HER) to lower the overpotentials in order to improve energy efficiency throughout the whole water splitting.6-8 It is well known that Pt and Ru/Ir oxides are traditionally the most effective catalysts for HER and OER, respectively.9-11 However, the low abundance and high cost of these noble metal limit their use in large-scale applications. Thus many efforts have been made to explore noble metal free and earth-abundant compounds as electrocatalysts for HER and OER.12-19 In recent years, the development of bifuctional electrocatalyst has gradually become the focus of attention，which can simplify the process and reduce the cost for overall water splitting.20-23 However, the researchers mainly focus on alkaline media, there are few reports on bifunctional catalysts under neutral conditions. In practical applications, the development of efficient bifunctional catalysts under neutral conditions can avoid the use of strong acids and bases that can pollute the environment and  thus improve the biocompatibility, which has far-reaching significance both in the environmental and economic benefits.
Polyoxometalates (POMs) are a class of well-defined early transition metal-oxo clusters. Due to their proton and electron reservoir abilities allowing electron and proton burst transmissions, POMs represent ideal catalysts for both oxidation and reduction processes.24-28 Most POMs contain some transition metals such as Ni, Cu, Zn, Co, Mo, etc, which show good OER and HER properties. Recently, Li and co-workers synthesized a series of POMs-derived nanohybrides with highly efficient performances for HER.29,30 A desired  OER electrocatalyst by directly carbonizing the Co-doped POMs/conductive polymer/graphene oxide precursors was systhezied by Lan and co-workers.31 However, the development of POMs-based materials as bifuctional electrocatalysts for overall water splitting still remains a challenge, especially in neutral medium.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a kind of porous crystal with extended three-dimensional framework, have excellent properties as new precursors for carbon-based catalytic materials in electrocatalytic reactions.32,33 As a class of MOFs, the zeolite imidazolide skeleton (ZIF) enriched in elemental N can be used as a sacrificial template for the preparation of N-doped carbon materials by pyrolysis in which the transition metals can react with N and C atoms to form transition metal-nitrogen-carbon structures as active sites for catalytic reactions.34,35 In addtion, permanent porosity and high surface area provides a great advantage as an excellent electrocatalyst for ZIFs materials. Herein, using POMs, ZIF-67 and dicyandiamide (DCD) as precursors, we prepare a novel bi-phase structure of cobalt and molybdenum carbide coated with nitrogen-doped graphite $\mathbf{\left(Co-Mo_{2}C@N C\right)}$ ). The synergistic effect between two metal components and the introduction of non-metallic  compositions can tune the electronic properties of the composite materials which improves electrocatalytic water splitting properties under alkaline conditions. More importantly, the catalyst has shown up an efficient performance under mild neutral conditions.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis
Carbon cloth (CC) was purchased from Jiangyin Youkun Experimental Equipment Co. Ltd. Nafion solution was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Other reagents were purchased from Xilong Chemical Co. Ltd. All chemicals were used as received, except CC. In a typical procedure, hydrophilic carbon cloth was obtained by refluxing it in concentrated ${\displaystyle{\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}}$ at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ , then cleaned with ethanol and water, and dried under Ar.
Synthesis of POM $@$ ZIF67@DCD: $\mathrm{{\bfH}}_{3}\mathrm{{PO}_{4}}$ · 12MoO3 (0.046 g), (Co(NO3)2· $\mathrm{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.873 g) and dicyandiamide (DCD, 2 g) were suspended in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol solution and heated to $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . 2-methylimidazole $(0.942\ \mathrm{g})$ was then added to the solution. The mixture was stirred for hours and washed with methanol three times to obtain purple precipitates. The obtained precipitates were dried at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for one night and then cooled down to room temperature.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C}@\mathbf{NC}$ : The samples of prepared POM@ZIF67@DCD were heated at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $120~\mathrm{min}$ in a static Ar atmosphere, and then naturally cooled to ambient temperature under Ar to obtain the desired black powder.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{PM_{012}}$ -ZIF67: The composite was prepared according to the same method of Co-Mo2C@NC except the addition of DCD.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{NC}$ : The composite was prepared according to the same method of Co-Mo2C@NC except the addition of ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}{\cdot}12{\mathrm{MoO}}_{3}$ .   
Synthesis of $\mathbf{Mo_{2}C}@\mathbf{NC}$ : The composite was prepared according to the same method of Co-Mo2C@NC except the addition of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and 2-methylimidazole. To prepare the working electrodes, the obtained products were dissolved in ethanol and sonicated for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to form a homogeneous ink. Then the catalyst ink was dropped directly onto the treated carbon cloth to form the catalyst film (the loading is $1\ \mathrm{mg\cm^{-2}},$ ). After drying under air, $20~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}~0.5~{\up w\upprime\%}$ Nafion solution (isopropanol as the solvent) was added onto its surface.
## 2.2 Characterization
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were performed on a JEM-2100F transmission electron microscopy at an acceleration voltage of $200\ \mathrm{kV}.$ Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained by a Hitachi model SU8020 FESEM. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were performed in ultrahigh vacuum (UHV) with a Krato, AXIS-HS monochromatized Al $\mathrm{Ka}$ cathode source, at 75-150 W, using a low energy electron gun for charge neutralization. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was carried out on a Bruker AXS D advance powder diffractometer equipped with a Cu Kα ( $\uplambda=$ 0.15418 nm). The Raman test was performed on a Renishaw Raman test system using a wavelength of $532\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.3 Electrochemistry
HER and OER electrochemical measurements were performed with a standard three-electrode system using a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation (CHI Instrument, Inc.) at room temperature. For the HER, the prepared Co-Mo2C@NC/CC film was used as the working electrode; a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and a graphite rod were used as the reference electrode and the counter electrode, respectively. The electrolyte was saturated with ultrahigh-pure Ar for $30~\mathrm{min}$ before cyclic voltammetry (CV) and linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurements. The performance of the $\mathbf{C}_{0}\mathbf{-M}_{0_{2}}\mathbf{C}_{\mathbf{\lambda}}@\mathbf{N}\mathbf{C}$ for OER was also tested using a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) as the reference electrode, and a Pt plate as the counter electrode. The electrolyte was saturated with ultrahigh-pure $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ before CV and LSV measurements. We investigated the HER and OER activity of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C}@\mathbf{NC}$ in 0.2 M PBS and 0.1 M KOH electrolytes. In $0.2{\bf M}$ PBS ( $\mathrm{pH}{=}7$ ), E (RHE) = E (SCE) + 0.659 V. In 0.1M KOH ( $\mathrm{\pH}{=}13$ ), E (RHE) $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ E (SCE) + 0.99 V. To test the long-time durability of the catalyst for HER and OER, controlled potential coulometry measurement was performed after the reduction potential was set.
For overall water splitting tests, the prepared Co-Mo2C@NC/CC electrodes in two-electrode water splitting system were used as both the anode and cathode electrodes, the electrolyte was saturated with ultrahigh-purity Ar for $30\ \mathrm{\min}$ before LSV and controlled-potential electrolysis method.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterizations
Scheme 1 illustrates the fabrication of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ which involved various steps and chemical transformations from POM@ZIF67@DCD to the final catalyst. As shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information), the XRD patterns confirm the formation of ZIF-67 in the precursor POM@ZIF67 $@$ DCD. And through high temperature carbonization, the final product $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ formed.
$\mathrm{H_{3}P M o_{12}O_{40}}$ $\mathrm{\bf{PMo}}_{12}\mathrm{\backslash}$ ), as a famous Keggin-type heteropolyanionic polyoxometalate, has the advantages of strong redox ability, water solubility and nanostructure. It can be uniformly dispersed in methanol solution. Here $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}$ can also be used as a source of metal Mo element and other non-metallic elements. When ZIF-67 was introduced and calcined with $\mathrm{\PMo}_{12}$ , the TEM image of formed $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}$ -ZIF67 particles is shown in Figure S2. As can be seen from the figure, a large number of irregular geometric shapes are stacked together; this structure should be attributed to the formed of ZIF-67. However, the aggregation of such large particles is obviously detrimental to the catalytic reaction process, because it greatly reduced the dispersion of reactive sites.
Therefore, the use of dinitrilediamines, namely DCD, is of crucial importance in the synthesis of the highly efficient bifunctional catalyst $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ since DCD can not only disperse the formed catalyst units, but also in situ generate the graphitic carbon species coupled with Co- $-\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ NPs. The formation of N-doped graphite carbon shell prevents $\mathrm{Co-Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles from coalescing and etchings, thereby increasing the electrocatalytic durability throughout the reaction.29 TEM images of Co-Mo2C@NC particles are shown in Figure 1. It can be observed from Figure 1a that the superstructure of catalyst has a number of particles range widely from about $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ to 100 nm, whereas the underlying structure of the catalyst resembles a sheet-like flexible substrate. The high resolution TEM (HRTEM) image as shown in Figure 2b indicates that three distinct lattice distances are evident. Among them, the lattice fringe with interplanar distance of $0.21~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is associated with the (112) carbides planes of Mo2C. The lattice plane distance of the Co is $0.18~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with the lattice plane (200). The lattice spacing of $0.34~\mathrm{nm}$ observed corresponds to the typical distance of graphitic carbon. The elemental mappings of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ further confirmed that the nanoparticles formed are comprised of Co elements. The element of C, N and Mo are evenly distributed throughout the structure.
From the TEM results we can conclude that the as-prepared $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ is a kind of wrap, bi-phase structure of cobalt and molybdenum carbide, which is coated with nitrogen-doped graphite sheet. The synergistic effect between the mixed $\mathrm{Co/\Mo_{2}C}$ NPs and nitrogen-doped graphite result in remarkable performance of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ .
Figure 2a is an X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ powder. The peaks clearly observed at 44.2 °, 51.5 ° and 75.8 ° are ascribed to the (111), (200) and (220) diffractions of Co (JCPDS, No. 15-0806), respectively. The peaks at 24.2 °, 39.5 ° and 42.4 ° are attributed to the (110), (121) and (112) planes of $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ (JCPDS, No. 72-1683). A wider peak observed around $10^{\circ}$ corresponds to the (002) plane of carbon in graphitic carbon. Thus the results from the PXRD are in consistent with the results obtained from the HRTEM in Figure 1. The Raman spectrum further confirms the graphitization degree of the catalyst as shown in Figure 2b. The peak at $1345~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the D band and G band of graphitic carbon, respectively. The ratio of $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ is 1.05, indicating that the material is partially graphitized and the internal degree of defects and disorder degree is larger, which is beneficial to the transfer of electrons. 30
XPS survey spectrum of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ shows C and N signals, as well as Co and Mo signals. For C, peaks located at 284.8, 285.6 and 289.4 eV are assigned to C=C/C-C, C-N and $0-C=0$ , respectively (Figure 3a). The existence of C-N demonstrates the heteroatom N-doping in the graphite. Figure 3b is the high resolution XPS spectrum of N 1s, revealing that N exits in two forms: pyridinic N located at $398.6\mathrm{eV}$ and graphitic N located at 401.3 eV. This result further confirms that N is successfully doped in the graphite which can change the internal electronic structure of the material to improve the electrochemical catalytic performance of the catalyst. Co 2p spectrum can be deconvoluted into two spin-orbit coupling and one shake-up satellites (Figure 3c). The peaks at 778.4 and 793.4 eV are attributable to the $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ core levels of Co whereas the peaks at 781.2 and 796.1 eV are assigned to the $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ core levels of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ . The presence of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ may be due to the oxidation of the material during placement or testing. Figure 3d shows the XPS spectrum of Mo 3d. The peak at $228.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ belongs to ${\mathrm{Mo}}^{2+}$ and can be contributed to the formation of $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ . At the same time, due to the surface oxidation, the Mo also has other two oxidation states. The peak at $229.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ belongs to $\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}$ and peaks at $232.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ 235.7 eV belong to Mo6+.
3.2 Overall water splitting performance in neutral condition
Based on the results of the above characterizations, we successfully obtained a stable N-doped graphite carbon coated $_{\mathrm{Co-Mo_{2}C}}$ catalyst, in which N-doped carbon shell and Mo-based nanostructures have good HER catalytic activity, 31, 36-39 while the synergistic effect between the mixed $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C}$ NPs may improve both the HER and OER performances. The synergistic effect of the above functional groups will ultimately enhance the overall performance of the water splitting reaction. Before investigating the catalyst as both the cathode and anode in a two-electrode system for overall water splitting, the electro-catalytic activities of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ towards HER and OER were first studied in neutral medium. All the tests were carried out on a carbon cloth (CC) substrate. The catalyst was dropped on the CC with the loading of $\mathrm{1mg~cm^{-2}}$ . Figure S3 is the SEM picture of the as-prepared Co-Mo2C@NC/CC film. It can be observed that the $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ nanoclusters are densely attached on the surface of CC, exhibiting sheet-like morphology. Since the catalyst is not soluble in ethanol, there appears some irregular particles material after dropping the catalyst onto the carbon cloth.
Figure 4a shows the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of Co-Mo2C@NC/CC for the HER in a 0.2 M PBS $\mathrm{\boldmath~\pH=}7\$ ) solution using a typical three-electrode configuration. For comparison, $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}$ -ZIF67, Co@NC, Mo2C@NC, blank CC and commercial Pt/C were also tested. As expected, Pt/C exhibits the best performance with an overpotential close to zero, it requires the overpotential of only $40~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve current density of $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . However, the blank CC shows negligible HER activity. As for the prepared Co-Mo2C@NC/CC, it exhibits remarkable HER activity with a sharply increased cathodic current. The overpotentials required for j values of 10 and $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ are 260 and $382\mathrm{mV},$ 。 respectively, superior to those of PMo $_{12}$ -ZIF67, Co@NC and $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{NC}$ . The corresponding Tafel slope is 118 mV/dec. These results highlight the significant increase in the catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ formed after the introduction of DCD with $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}$ -ZIF67 since DCD can not only disperse the formed catalyst units, but also in situ generate the graphitic carbon species coupled with $_{\mathrm{Co-Mo_{2}C}}$ NPs. The synergistic effect between N-doped graphite carbon shell and bi-phase structure of $_{\mathrm{Co-Mo_{2}C}}$ contributes to the improvement of HER performance. In addition, Co-Mo2C@NC/CC displays an excellent stability as revealed by controlled potential electrolysis for more than 16 hours at $\mathrm{\Delta\eta=300~mV}$ (Figure S4a). The current increases during the electrolysis process, indicating the activation of the catalyst during the long term stability test.
The Co-Mo2C@NC/CC electrode was further explored with respect to the electrocatalytic OER in the same media. The Co-Pi/CC catalyst, PMo $12$ -ZIF67, Co $@$ NC and $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{NC}$ as well as blank CC was also tested for comparison. Figure 4c shows the polarization curves of the different anodes at neutral conditions with iR correction. As expected, blank CC shows very poor OER activity and the Co-Pi/CC catalyst is active for OER, with overpotentials of 620 mV for $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , close to the literature values.32-34 Noticeably, the OER performance of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ and $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}$ -ZIF67 is significantly improved, requiring only an overpotential of $440~\mathrm{mV}$ and $520~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve a catalytic current density of 10 mA cm-2. As for $\operatorname{Co}\ @\operatorname{NC}$ and $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{NC}$ , the corresponding potentials at the current density of $10~\mathrm{\mA~\cm^{-2}}$ are more positve than that for the $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ . The better performance of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ can also be shown via Tafel slope calculations ( $\mathrm{156~mV},$ /dec for $\mathrm{Co-Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{CC}$ and $191~\mathrm{mV}.$ /dec for $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}$ -ZIF67/CC), which evidences that $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ has a more favourable catalytic kinetics for OER. These results are consistent with the previous HER results. The long-term stability of the anode can also be characterized by bulk electrolysis at a fixed overpotential of $400\ \mathrm{mV}.$ As shown in Figure S4b, the $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ electrode maintains its activity, the current remaining constant for several hours.
Based on the good activity of $\mathbf{Co-Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@$ NC for HER and OER, we directly assembled a two-electrode cell system using $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ as the cathode and anode in $0.2{\bf M}$ PBS $\mathrm{\langlepH}=7\cdot$ ) solution for overall water splitting. In addition, $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\|\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}$ and CC‖CC were also assembled and tested for comparison. As shown in Figure 5a, a current density of $10~\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C\|C o-M o_{2}C@N C}$ can be achieved at a cell voltage of $1.83~\mathrm{V},$ at the same time both the cathode and anode of carbon cloth surface have obvious bubble generated. In the low voltage region, the current for Co-Mo2C@NC‖Co-Mo2C@NC is lower than that of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\|\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}$ , but exceeds it in the high voltage region. The CC‖CC shows negligible activity as we expected. The long-term stability test at a cell voltage of 1.65 V is shown in Figure 5b, the current is stable for few hours and has an increasing trend which further demonstrates the excellent stability of the pair of electrodes. Table S1 (Supporting Information) compares the performances of different bifunctional catalysts for overall water splitting at neutral or near-neutral conditions, showing that $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ exhibits excellent activity among all the reported systems.
In order to understand the change of structure for the catalyst coated on CC after HER and OER tests, SEM and XPS analysis have been performed. The SEM images of the Co-Mo2C@NC/CC hybrids after electrolysis at overpotential of $300~\mathrm{mV}$ for HER and 400 mV for OER in neutral conditions are represented on Figure S5. From these images it is clearly observed that in both cases, the morphology of the $\mathrm{Co-Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC/CC}$ turned from many sheet-like pieces into a kind of dense and uniform shell. Moreover, after electrolysis for HER, small nanoparticles appeared with the size of several tens of nanometres. However, after the electrolysis for OER, the catalyst turned into dense and smooth shell wrapped on carbon cloth, and from the crack we can predict that the catalyst shell has a certain thickness. These findings thus evidence that the Co-Mo2C@NC/CC acts as the catalyst for both reactions, the in-situ formed nanostructures being different for HER and OER.
As shown in Figure S6a of XPS tests, after electrolysis for HER, we could not observe the peak of $\mathbf{Mo}^{2+}$ . The peaks at 232.5 and $235.6\mathrm{~eV}$ are attributable to the $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{1/2}$ core levels of $\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}$ whereas the peaks at 233.6 and $236.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{1/2}$ core levels of $\mathbf{Mo}^{6+}$ . The concentration of $\mathrm{Mo}^{4+}$ is greater than $\mathbf{Mo}^{6+}$ , indicating that most of the $\mathbf{Mo}^{6+}$ is reduced to Mo4+. In addition, as shown in Figure S6b, the element of N also changed which only left pyridinic N located at $398.7\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Besides, XPS analysis also indicates that the peaks related to Co and C have only very minor changes.
As indicated above, after the electrolysis for OER, the catalyst turned into dense and smooth shell wrapped on carbon cloth (Figure S5b), with certain thickness. XPS analysis shows that the content of molybdenum is very low, suggesting that the $_{\mathrm{Co-Mo_{2}C}}$ undergoes structural transformation and decomposition during the OER process, while the molybdenum was dissolved in the electrolyte. The change of $\mathbf{N}$ is like what to be after HER test, but the location of pyridinic N shifted positively. In contrast, the peaks related to the other two elements (C, Co) are only slightly affected. All these findings suggest that the $_{\mathrm{Co-Mo_{2}C}}$ catalyst in-situ formed nanostructures being different for HER and OER.
3.3 Overall water splitting performance in alkaline condition After studying the overall water splitting performance of the Co-Mo2C@NC as a bifunctional catalyst under neutral conditions, we investigated the performance of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ catalyst under alkaline conditions, where 0.1 M KOH electrolyte was used as the electrolyte for all electrochemical measurements. Similar to the research under neutral conditions, we first studied the HER and OER properties of the catalyst. As shown in Figure 6a, the $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ also shows significant HER activity under alkaline conditions. The cathode current increases dramatically. The corresponding overpotential at the current densities of 10 mA cm- and $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ are $225\mathrm{mV}$ and $392{\mathrm{~mV}},$ respectively. The Tafel slope of this catalyst is 178 mV/dec (as shown in Figure 6a inset). It can be seen from Figure S7a that $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ can maintain the stability up to several hours at η=300 mV during the control voltage electrolysis and the current density can reach up to $13\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . During the electrolysis process, the current density shows an increasing trend, indicating that the catalyst remains active for a long time during the test. Figure 6b shows the LSV curves of OER for $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ under alkaline conditions. The overpotentials required for j values of 10 and $50\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ are 370 and $400~\mathrm{{mV}},$ , respectively. It can be seen from Figure S7b that the current can maintain several hours of stability at the overpotential of $370~\mathrm{mV}$ during control voltage electrolysis and the current density can reach to a value of $8\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ . However, during the electrolysis, the current density increased first and then decreased again, indicating a certain loss of catalyst activity over a long period of time.
In addition we also assembled the battery cell under alkaline conditions and tested the overall water splitting reaction performance. As shown in Figure $\mathrm{7a}$ , the potential required for the $\mathbf{C}_{0}\mathbf{-M}_{0_{2}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{\mathcal{Q}}$ NC‖Co-Mo2C@NC to reach a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and 50 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is 1.66 V and 1.90 V, respectively. As for the $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\|\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}$ couples, the potential required to reach 10 mA cm-2  is 1.59 V. However, the potential required to reach the current density of 50 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is 1.95 V, which is higher than that for Co-Mo2C@NC‖Co-Mo2C@NC. These results indicate that in high voltage region, the Co-Mo2C@NC‖Co-Mo2C@NC couple is more favourable, whether it is under neutral or alkaline conditions. Figure 7b is the time-current curve at the applied potential of $1.7\mathrm{~V~}$ which reflects the stability of the system.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, we have developed an efficient bifunctional electrocatalyst for overall water splitting both in neutral and alkaline media. The calcination of the POM $@$ ZIF-67 and DCD generates Co-Mo2C@NC nanohybrids which are responsible for  high performance of HER and OER catalyst driving $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at 260 and $400~\mathrm{{mV}}$ in neutral medium, respectively. The two-electrode system using $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C@N C}$ as both the anode and cathode drives $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at a cell voltage of $1.83\mathrm{~V~}$ with strong long-term stability. To best of our knowledge, this is the first time that, using POM as precursors to prepare such active overall water splitting electrocatalyst in neutral media. Besides, the catalyst also possesses remarkable performances in alkaline medium. The high activity for 1 $\mathbf{\chi}_{\mathbf{O}-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{\mathcal{Q}}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{C}}$ can be due to the synergistic effect between N-doped graphite carbon shell and bi-phase structure of $\mathbf{Co-Mo_{2}C}$ and the increase of catalytic sites. This work can open new floodgates to explore such non-noble metal electrode materials to achieve highly efficient energy storage and conversion systems.
